(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-4-transfusion-medicine-blood-bank-quiz"]={"title":"Transfusion Medicine and Blood Banking Quiz 1 — Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with a known red cell alloantibody is scheduled for elective hip surgery. When should her type and screen be ordered?","choices":["Well before the surgery date","When she starts to bleed","After the crossmatch is done","On the morning of surgery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When a screen is positive, finding compatible antigen-negative units can take hours or days, so type and screen is ordered well in advance of elective procedures. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 12 and 15"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man of unknown blood type needs a massive transfusion after a fall. Which red cells are standard for him?","choices":["AB-positive","O-negative","Type-specific units","O-positive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. O-positive is abundant (about 37 to 39% of people) and is the massive transfusion standard for adult males and post-menopausal females. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man gets high fever, rigors, flank pain and red urine minutes into a transfusion. Which laboratory pattern is expected?","choices":["Absent haptoglobin, high lactate dehydrogenase","High tryptase with anti-IgA","Normal haptoglobin and lactate dehydrogenase","Spherocytes with a new anti-Jka"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute hemolytic reaction: undetectable haptoglobin, markedly elevated LDH (lactate dehydrogenase) and later a rise in indirect bilirubin, with pink or red plasma and red urine. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 17 and 22"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man's washed red cells agglutinate with anti-A, do not agglutinate with anti-B, and agglutinate with anti-D. What is his type?","choices":["Group A, RhD-negative","Group B, RhD-positive","Group A, RhD-positive","Group AB, RhD-positive"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Forward typing reads antigens: agglutination with anti-A means the A antigen is present, no reaction with anti-B means no B antigen, and agglutination with anti-D means RhD-positive. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man's transfusion has just been stopped for a suspected reaction. How is his intravenous access kept?","choices":["Fresh saline through new tubing","Flush the old line with saline","Remove the catheter at once","Restart the unit more slowly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Maintain intravenous access by hanging fresh normal saline with new tubing; never flush the remaining blood in the transfusion line into the patient. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 16 and 20"},{"q":"A 31-year-old RhD-negative woman without anti-D has just delivered an RhD-positive baby. When should she receive Rh immune globulin?","choices":["At her next prenatal visit","If the baby becomes jaundiced","Within 72 hours of delivery","Not needed after a first birth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The post-partum dose is given within 72 hours of delivering an RhD-positive neonate, adding to the routine dose at about 28 weeks. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man's type and crossmatch has tagged and reserved 2 units, but surgery is canceled. What happens to the units?","choices":["They are converted to plasma","They expire after 72 hours","Nobody else can use them until released","They go back to stock at once"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Held units are unavailable to other patients until explicitly released, which is why a type and screen, which holds none, preserves inventory when transfusion is unlikely. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman's plasma agglutinates reagent A1 cells but not reagent B cells. What is her ABO group?","choices":["Group O","Group A","Group B","Group AB"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Reverse typing reads antibodies: agglutination of A1 cells only means anti-A is present, the pattern of group B, whose plasma holds anti-A. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a febrile non-hemolytic reaction during platelets. What causes this kind of reaction?","choices":["ABO IgM with complement lysis","Stored cytokines or anti-white cell antibodies","Anti-IgA in an IgA-deficient patient","Recipient IgE to donor plasma proteins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The febrile non-hemolytic reaction has a dual cause: cytokines (interleukin-1, interleukin-6, tumor necrosis factor alpha) accumulated in stored plasma, and recipient antibodies against donor white cell antigens. It is commoner with platelets. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 19, 20, 17 and 21"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has hives during a transfusion. What do his direct antiglobulin test and hemolysis labs show?","choices":["Positive direct test, normal labs","Negative direct test, normal labs","Positive direct test, spherocytes","Negative direct test, high lactate dehydrogenase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Laboratory evaluation in the allergic reaction is normal: the direct antiglobulin test is negative and there are no hemolysis markers. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 20 and 22"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man gets hives during a platelet transfusion; it is paused and an antihistamine given, but he then begins wheezing. What should happen to the unit?","choices":["Do not restart it","Restart it after a second dose","Restart it more slowly","Finish it within 4 hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Critical red flag: if fever, dyspnea, wheezing or hypotension occur, do NOT restart; escalate to the full reaction protocol. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man has a transfusion reaction with fever, stable blood pressure, a negative direct antiglobulin test and clear plasma and urine. Which reaction fits?","choices":["Anaphylactic","Allergic","Acute hemolytic","Febrile non-hemolytic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fever with no hypotension, a negative direct test and normal hemolysis labs (clear plasma and urine) is the febrile non-hemolytic fingerprint, a diagnosis of exclusion. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man transfused 6 weeks ago has a type and screen drawn. His surgery is 5 days later. What is needed before surgery?","choices":["A new sample, drawn and retested","An electronic crossmatch","Nothing; the sample lasts a week","A repeat forward type"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. After a transfusion within the prior 3 months the sample is valid for only 72 hours (3 days), because new alloantibodies can form rapidly after secondary exposure; an expired sample needs a new draw and repeat testing. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman is starting her first unit of red cells. How should the first 25 to 50 mL be given?","choices":["As a rapid bolus to check tolerance","Slowly, while she is watched closely","After routine premedication","At the full ordered rate from the start"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The first 15 minutes are critical: infuse the first 25 to 50 mL slowly, because a severe acute hemolytic reaction shows itself rapidly, after as little as 25 mL. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 4, 17 and 19"},{"q":"A 27-year-old RhD-negative woman without anti-D has abdominal trauma late in pregnancy with a large fetal-maternal bleed. Which test decides whether she needs more than the usual Rh immune globulin?","choices":["Antibody identification panel","Reverse typing","Direct antiglobulin test","Kleihauer-Betke test"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The Kleihauer-Betke test quantifies the volume of fetal-maternal hemorrhage, to calculate whether additional vials of Rh immune globulin are required. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 9, 14 and 12"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman's antibody screen is negative. Outside the ABO system, which red cell antigen is the most immunogenic?","choices":["Kidd","Kell","RhD","Duffy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. RhD is the most immunogenic red cell antigen outside the ABO system, a transmembrane protein; an Rh-negative person exposed by transfusion or pregnancy can form anti-D. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 8 and 12"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man's type is unknown and he needs red cells before typing can be done. Which ABO group is the universal red cell donor?","choices":["Group A","Group AB","Group O","Group B"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Group O red cells carry no A or B antigen, so recipient anti-A and anti-B have nothing to react with: O is the universal red cell donor. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 25-year-old pregnant woman has her routine prenatal antibody screen. Which test is this?","choices":["Forward typing","Kleihauer-Betke test","Indirect antiglobulin test","Direct antiglobulin test"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The indirect antiglobulin test (indirect Coombs) looks for free, unbound antibodies in plasma; its uses include maternal prenatal antibody screens, pre-transfusion screening and crossmatching. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 14 and 9"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man is about to receive a red cell unit. The nurses check his name, medical record number, the unit's blood type and its serial number. Against what is this information matched directly?","choices":["The room number on the door","The electronic order alone","His spoken date of birth","His wristband"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two-identifier verification matches the patient's name, medical record number, the unit's blood type and the unit's serial number directly against the patient's wristband at the bedside. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with a known anti-Kell needs 2 units. Which crossmatch tier takes the longest per unit?","choices":["Each tier takes equal time","Immediate-spin crossmatch","Electronic crossmatch","Full antiglobulin crossmatch"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The full antiglobulin crossmatch, which he needs, takes 45 to 60 or more minutes per unit (37 °C incubation, then antihuman globulin); immediate spin takes about 5 to 10 minutes and electronic under a minute. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman of unknown blood type is bleeding heavily after a car crash and needs red cells immediately. Which units should be released?","choices":["Uncrossmatched O-positive","Group AB-negative","Uncrossmatched O-negative","Units after a full crossmatch"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. O-negative is the scarce universal red cell, kept as a strict emergency reserve and mandatory for females of childbearing potential (under 50) with an unknown type, so RhD exposure cannot sensitize her. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 7, 6 and 13"},{"q":"A 56-year-old woman dies after a hemolytic transfusion reaction. Where does such a fatal reaction most often begin?","choices":["A clerical error at the bedside","A missed antibody on the screen","Contamination of the donor unit","A laboratory typing failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Human error drives mortality: most fatal hemolytic reactions stem from bedside clerical errors, not from laboratory testing. Verify the patient and the unit at the bedside. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 24, 4 and 17"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man's peripheral smear is reviewed 10 days after a transfusion with a falling hemoglobin. Which smear finding fits a delayed hemolytic reaction?","choices":["Blast cells","A normal smear","Eosinophils","Spherocytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Delayed hemolysis is extravascular: splenic macrophages clear IgG-coated cells, and the peripheral smear shows spherocytes, with elevated LDH (lactate dehydrogenase) and indirect bilirubin and reduced haptoglobin. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man who types as group AB needs red cells. From which ABO groups can he receive them?","choices":["AB only","AB, A, B or O","A or B only","O only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Group AB plasma has no ABO antibodies, so red cells of any ABO group are safe: AB is the universal red cell recipient. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman with sickle cell disease has a delayed reaction in which both the donor cells and her own red cells are destroyed and her reticulocytes fall. Why is this dangerous?","choices":["It needs washed red cells next","It needs Rh immune globulin","It will resolve with antihistamine","More transfusion can be fatal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hyperhemolysis syndrome: the antibodies destroy BOTH donor and autologous red cells, reticulocytopenia develops, and further transfusions can be fatal. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 18, 21, 20 and 9"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman types as group B. Which enzyme gives her red cells their ABO antigen?","choices":["N-acetylgalactosamine transferase","D-galactosyltransferase","A transmembrane D protein","No transferase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Group B possesses D-galactosyltransferase and group A possesses N-acetylgalactosamine transferase; group O shows only the unmodified H core, with no A or B sugars. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 5 and 8"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with severe IgA deficiency and anti-IgA survived transfusion anaphylaxis. What must she receive for future transfusions?","choices":["Leukoreduced red cells","Washed red cells","Routine premedication","Group AB plasma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Future transfusions must be washed red cells, which strips the plasma IgA, or products sourced from verified IgA-deficient donors. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 21, 19 and 6"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman's temperature rises 2.3 °C during a platelet transfusion and her blood pressure falls. Beyond the hemolysis workup, what must be investigated?","choices":["A delayed hemolytic reaction","Bacterial sepsis from the product","A new anti-Kell antibody","An allergic reaction to plasma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rule out sepsis: if the fever is 2 °C or more, the patient is hypotensive, or the product was platelets, work up for bacterial sepsis (blood bag Gram stain and culture). She has all three. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 19 and 18"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man receives ABO-incompatible red cells and his cells lyse inside the circulation within minutes. Which antibody class drives this?","choices":["IgE","IgA","IgG","IgM"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ABO antibodies are IgM: large pentamers that are highly efficient at fixing complement (C1q binding), so the membrane attack complex lyses cells directly in the circulation. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 5 and 10"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has a negative antibody screen, no history of significant antibodies, and only one blood type record on file. Which crossmatch fits?","choices":["Immediate-spin crossmatch","A direct antiglobulin test","Full antiglobulin crossmatch","Electronic crossmatch"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Immediate spin needs a negative screen with no history of clinically significant antibodies; her serum is mixed with donor cells at room temperature and centrifuged at once, in about 5 to 10 minutes. — 21. Transfusion Medicine Blood Banking Fundamentals SV.pptx, Slides 13 and 14"}]};
